WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.310
Every year has its fair share of highs and
lows at the movies, but some always seem to

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:05.920
stand taller than the others.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.000
With the benefit of 20 years of hindsight,
1999 looks like one of cinema's greatest ever

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.420
years and each of these films is well worth
rediscovering as they reach their milestone

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.240
anniversaries.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.780
These are some of the best movies turning
20 in 2019.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.380
As the 20th century drew to a close, society
seemed to be spinning headlong into something

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.700
of an identity crisis.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.600
It's only fitting, then, that 1999 was also
the year that Fight Club brawled its way into

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:35.640
theaters, and left the world asking: "did
you know the truth about Tyler Durden?"

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.260
Unless you were a fan of Chuck Palahniuk's
original book, the answer to that question

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.240
was likely no.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.140
Fight Club’s twist is one for the history
books, and has got to be one of the main reasons

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.940
why watching David Fincher's film adaptation
unfold was so much fun.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.699
Part scathing indictment of corporate and
consumer culture, part deconstruction of modern

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:58.060
masculine mythos and part exploration of white
male privilege, Fincher's film is a take-no-prisoners

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:03.460
look at a society on the brink of self-destruction
— and in desperate need of a little anarchy.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.700
Fronted by Edward Norton, Brad Pitt and a
marvelously manic Helena Bonham-Carter, anarchy

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.090
is just what Fincher and crew deliver with
the brutal and bleak bare-knuckle bout that

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:18.800
is Fight Club — which remains just as potent
and full of surprises 20 years after its release.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.440
Though it takes a radically different approach
to the subject matter, Mike Judge's office

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.710
comedy Office Space seems largely cut from
the same thematic cloth as Fight Club.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.630
After all, they're both concerned with the
soul-crushing materialism of society, and

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.640
both follow an office drone so drained by
everyday life that they become desperate to

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.580
find a way out.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:43.500
Rather than allow his hypnotist-fractured
psyche to start an underground fight-club-slash-anarchist-revolution,

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.470
Judge's protagonist takes a slightly less
violent approach to personal anarchy — he

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.670
enlists his buddies in a scheme to shake down
the soulless corporation he works for.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.220
If you've seen Office Space, you’ll know
how hilariously wrong that scheme goes, but

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.940
the film isn't really too concerned with the
heist itself.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.240
Instead, Judge uses the setting and plot of
Office Space to spin a not-so-satirical, laugh-out-loud

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.850
farce about how effortlessly corporations
and consumerism devour individuality.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:12.231
And while condemning that culture, Office
Space is also the rare kind of film which

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:16.450
also understands that many people are just
fine toiling away in the corporate digestive

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.390
system — so long as they get to keep their
sweet Swingline stapler — and that, if you

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.280
aren't one of them...

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.120
well, nobody's making you stay.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:32.000
“Oh, oh, and I almost forgot I’m also
gonna need you to come in on Sunday too, ‘kay?”

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.880
Spike Jonze’s wild, frenetic and endlessly
creative film Being John Malkovich may have

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:41.450
netted him an Oscar nomination for Best Director,
but it was hardly his first gig in the director's

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:41.940
chair.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.500
The adventurous filmmaker had spent the bulk
of the '90s honing his skills and developing

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.950
his style while directing music videos for
the likes of the Beastie Boys, Weezer, Sonic

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.680
Youth, Daft Punk, and Björk.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:58.260
Where some directors naturally gravitate toward
gritty realism and dense personal drama, Jonze

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.480
opts to take those same elements and bolster
them with visually arresting, semi-whimsical

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.950
narratives rife with absurdist flights of
fancy and breathtaking stylistic flourishes.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.530
Jonze's style proved more than ideal for Being
John Malkovich, which was scripted by Charlie

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.620
Kaufman the poet-laureate of the outlandish
and the eerily eccentric.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.210
Being John Malkovich, which follows the beautifully
bizarre tale of a puppeteer who discovers

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:25.610
a portal into the head of the titular thespian,
is dripping with fantastical flourishes from

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.300
both writer and director.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.940
It features show-stopping performances from
John Cusack, Cameron Diaz, Catherine Keener,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.010
and, of course, Malkovich himself.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.450
It also takes more creative and narrative
risks than most films ever dare; making it

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.720
not just one of the best films of 1999, but
also one of the all-time greats.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.720
It's almost impossible to imagine now, but
prior to the release of The Matrix almost

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:50.060
nobody knew who the Wachowskis [wuh-CHOW-skees]
were.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:53.670
After all, they had just two films to their
names, and one of them — the perfectly dreadful

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:58.640
Stallone-and-Banderas-led action flick Assassins
— had only earned them a mere shared screenwriting

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.140
credit.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.900
Even their sleek, inventive crime thriller
Bound went all but unnoticed upon release.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.520
Needless to say, expectations were practically
non-existent for the Wachowskis' follow-up,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.970
which was billed as a tech-savvy, effects-driven
action movie featuring Keanu Reeves.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:17.750
To say the Wachowskis seized the world’s
attention in March of 1999 would be a huge

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:18.400
understatement.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.140
Not only was The Matrix a heady, character-driven
tale of man versus technology run amok, it

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:28.620
was also a groundbreaking visual leap forward
that shaped sci-fi cinema for an entire generation

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.700
and became a creative and commercial success
that the Wachowskis could never quite repeat.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.880
They can at least take heart knowing that,
even 20 years after its release, The Matrix

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.980
really does feel as fresh and profound as
ever.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:41.220
“Woah.”

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.040
After delivering back-to-back stunners with
his first two films, director Paul Thomas

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.800
Anderson had many in the business hailing
him as the next Martin Scorsese.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.640
New Line Cinema stepped up to produce his
third, and he delivered a sprawling, $37 million

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.460
art film that followed a dozen or so loosely
connected lives through a single day — and

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.590
ever-changing weather patterns — in the
San Fernando Valley.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:07.639
Clocking in at a whopping three hours and
eight minutes, Magnolia marked Anderson's

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:10.360
most ambitious and personal project to date.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.010
It's a film that seamlessly combined the director's
penchant for high melodrama with his flair

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:19.290
for grandiose set pieces and meticulously
observed, character-driven narratives — all

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:24.240
set against a billboard-sized canvas, and
led by a near-supernatural sense of fate which

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.500
is barely even glimpsed in his earlier works.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.990
The result is a colorful mosaic of a movie
that proved to be one of 1999's most challenging

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:33.200
and rewarding releases.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.980
Of all the fantastic movies released in 1999,
few had quite the cultural impact enjoyed

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.780
by M. Night Shyamalan's spellbinding supernatural
thriller The Sixth Sense.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:47.300
Shyamalan's masterpiece arrived in theaters
under a shroud of mystery — but thanks to

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.530
a star turn from Bruce Willis, one of the
greatest child performances in history from

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:56.730
Haley Joel Osment and a mind-blowing twist
ending, it became a legitimate cultural phenomenon

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.440
almost overnight.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.100
“I see dead people.”

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:07.960
It also cleared well north of half a billion
dollars at the box office, earned six Academy

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:12.060
Award nominations, and made a household name
of the man who wrote and directed it.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:16.240
Over the years, Hollywood — and Shyamalan
himself — has repeatedly tried and mostly

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.700
failed to reproduce the bracing impact of
the film's twisty narrative.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.880
Because Shyamalan's vision is so skillfully
executed and vividly realized, The Sixth Sense

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:29.660
has only grown in esteem as a result, and
the film's hefty emotional impact still hits

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.140
home whether you know its secrets or not.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:35.560
In fact, part of the fun of rewatching The
Sixth Sense is searching for clues that you

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:38.800
missed the first three or four or even five
times through.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.760
But what's often overshadowed by the film's
twist is that, at its core, The Sixth Sense

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.640
is also a tenderly observed drama about grief
and loss, fronted by a troubled youth learning

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.580
to accept the burdens of a gift he never asked
for.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.680
That Shyamalan fitted such high drama into
a genuinely unsettling ghost story is what

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.460
makes The Sixth Sense the stuff of genre legend.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.220
Just a week prior to The Sixth Sense’s arrival
in theaters, another stunning supernatural

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.200
confection hit theaters in the form of The
Blair Witch Project.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.580
Conceived and directed by newcomers Daniel
Myrick and Eduardo Sanchez, and starring entirely

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:15.300
unknown actors, the groundbreaking no-budget
feature follows three film students who get

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:19.180
lost in the woods while shooting a documentary
about a small-town urban legend.

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:23.220
As they struggle to find their way back to
society, it becomes clear they're not alone

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.960
in the woods, and may be being stalked by
the Blair Witch herself.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.960
While that story would have made an interesting
enough horror film in its own right, it's

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.160
how Myrick and Sanchez executed and marketed
their story that really made the The Blair

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.440
Witch Project stand apart from pretty much
every found-footage flick made before — or

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:38.940
since.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.320
The directors essentially dropped their actors
off in the woods, orchestrated spooky events

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.760
on a daily basis, and had their performers
shoot the action themselves.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:51.500
The resulting film is gritty and patently
terrifying, and looks and feels just a little

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.500
too real for comfort.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.140
The filmmakers even doubled down on that realism
by setting up a genius marketing campaign

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:01.220
that sold the film to audiences as a real-life
documentary.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.320
The stark realism both baffled and enthralled
critics and audiences, which helped make The

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:08.540
Blair Witch Project one of the most profitable
movies ever produced.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:13.919
Whether you like his movies or not, Stanley
Kubrick is and always will be regarded as

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.960
one of the greatest directors that ever lived
if not the greatest.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:21.220
Whether it was any good or not, his final
film was destined to be one that cinephiles

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:26.420
world would pore over, debate, watch, and
rewatch until the end of time.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:30.280
To that end, Kubrick, who spent his career
testing the limits of cinema with narratively

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:34.589
challenging and morally ambiguous art films,
continued to push buttons from beyond the

00:08:34.589 --> 00:08:39.960
grave with his moody and masterfully-executed
swan song: Eyes Wide Shut.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:45.550
Based on a 1926 novella entitled Dream Story,
the film follows a man who ends up at this

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:51.640
crazy, kinky house where rich folks wear creepy
masks and do even creepier, kinkier things.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:57.180
And no, that summation doesn't quite illustrate
the enigmatic power of Kubrick's kinky, semi-surrealist

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:57.720
nightmare.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.199
It doesn't do justice to the complex personal
quandaries explored by the husband and wife

00:09:01.199 --> 00:09:05.879
in question, either, and nor does it divulge
the sheer amount of blood, sweat, and tears

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.480
put into the film's torturous, two-and-a-half
year production.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.640
While we may never see Kubrick's NC-17 final
cut of Eyes Wide Shut, the version which hit

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:21.000
theaters in 1999 remains a haunting last work
absolutely worth poring over, debating, watching,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.020
and rewatching.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.720
Tim Burton and Johnny Depp may have hit a
bit of a cold streak of late, but there was

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.560
once a time when that pairing was one of the
most exciting in Hollywood.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:33.000
So when they announced they were teaming up
for an adaptation of Washington Irving's short

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.460
story The Legend of Sleepy Hollow, there was
more than a little excitement around the project

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:41.160
— mostly because the story jibed so well
with Burton's ability to make the bizarre

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.680
and grotesque so endearing and entertaining.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.120
And that's exactly what Burton did with Sleepy
Hollow, which still remains a stunning work

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:48.900
of gothic whimsy.

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:53.460
A crackerjack piece of writing from Seven
scribe Andrew Kevin Walker is largely to thank

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:56.920
— Walker’s script transforms Irving's
brief and brutal Headless Horseman fable into

00:09:56.939 --> 00:10:01.649
a complex murder mystery, pitting the skittish
Ichabod Crane as detective on the hunt for

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:03.940
what appears to be a serial killer.

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:07.739
Of course, Crane finds something far more
sinister afoot in the town of Sleepy Hollow,

00:10:07.739 --> 00:10:12.040
and a never-better Depp helps draw viewers
further into the eerie unknown with a performance

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:16.840
that teeters between awkward charm, cagey
confidence, and nervy unease.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:21.819
Thanks to a beautifully bleak visual palette
courtesy of Emmanuel Lubezki, as well as unforgettable

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:27.800
appearances from Christina Ricci and Christopher
Walken, Burton and crew transformed Irving's

00:10:27.800 --> 00:10:32.520
classic into a gruesome, gothic fantasia that
stands as the pinnacle of the Burton/Depp

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:33.320
cinematic dynasty.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.520
